NOTICE — Nori A3 Description
==================================

WHAT THIS IS
------------
A description of the Nori A3 robot for use in simulation, visualization and
research: kinematics, approximate inertial properties, collision primitives, and
visual meshes.


NOT FOR SAFETY-CRITICAL USE
--------------------------
This model describes a machine with powered arms and a powered lift. It is a
simplified approximation intended for simulation, visualisation and research.

Do not use it to determine clearances, reach envelopes, stopping distances, load
capacity, stability or tip limits for any real robot, and do not use it as the
basis of a safety case or a risk assessment. Its inertial properties are
estimates, its collision geometry is deliberately coarser than the hardware, and
its total mass is knowingly below the real value (see ACCURACY).

Safety on a real Nori robot is enforced on the robot itself, not by anything in
this package.


NOT MANUFACTURING DATA
--------------

  * The meshes are simplified visual approximations. Internal geometry has been
    removed and surfaces have been decimated. 

  * There is no CAD in this package. 

  * Parts produced from these meshes will not fit, function, or be safe. Do not
    use them for manufacture, and do not rely on them for any dimension that
    matters.


ACCURACY
--------
  * Kinematics — joint axes, limits and link transforms — are measured and
    verified against hardware.

  * Inertial properties are APPROXIMATE. Masses are measured, but each link is
    modeled as a uniform-density primitive, so centers of mass and inertia
    tensors are estimates. They are suitable for kinematics, visualization and
    general-purpose simulation. They are NOT suitable for torque-level
    sim-to-real transfer or any safety-critical calculation.

  * Collision geometry is deliberately primitive (boxes, cylinders, spheres),
    not a faithful hull of the visual meshes.

  * THE LIFT'S JOINT LIMIT IS AN OPERATING CEILING, NOT THE STROKE. The
    mechanism's physical stroke is 720 mm; lift_extension_joint is limited to
    700 mm. The 20 mm reserve keeps the carriage off the top mechanical stop and
    leaves room for overshoot, and it is what the robot's own controllers and
    teleop tools command. Simulate against 700 mm and you match the hardware's
    behaviour; the extra 20 mm exists but is not commanded.

  * TOTAL MASS IS 8.93% LIGHT. The links sum to about 18.3 kg; a real robot
    weighs 20.5 kg. The difference is hardware not modelled as its own link -
    USB hubs, wiring loom, cable management, larger fasteners - and is assumed
    distributed in proportion to the rest. Scale by 1.121 for the real total.

  * The lift assembly total is measured; the split across stages is an
    engineering estimate, and may be cause for uncertainty in this model for stability work.

  * Camera POSITIONS are measured. Camera AIM angles are modelled, but may vary slightly due to material constraints.


THIRD-PARTY CONTENT
-------------------
  * The lidar is drawn as a PRIMITIVE STAND-IN, not the vendor's geometry. The
    real RPLIDAR C1 mesh is Slamtec's and is not ours to redistribute, so this
    bundle ships a stub built from two cylinders matched to the sensor's outer
    envelope. It is the right size and in the right place; it is not a model of
    the sensor. No third-party geometry is included in this bundle.


